n":"Sensitivity (blue circles) to the \\0 half-life of a nEXO-like experiment as a function of total background in \\Q$\\pm$FWHM/2 in the inner 2000 kg. All components of nEXO's background model except for the \\2 term are scaled to generate this curve. The red curve is the result of fitting the computed values with $T^{0\\nu}_{1/2}\\propto B^x$, giving $x=-0.35$.","filename":"figures_background.png"},{"key":"2b217c446bc2756ee79570d871ede5e6","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/2b217c446bc2756ee79570d871ede5e6","source":"arxiv","caption":"Median exclusion sensitivity at 90\\% C.L. and $3\\sigma$ discovery potential to the \\0 half-life of a nEXO-like experiment under a background consisting only of the \\2 component.","filename":"figures_livetime_Ba.png"},{"key":"24b5cc302350360550111a947ea5de45","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/24b5cc302350360550111a947ea5de45","source":"arxiv","caption":"90\\% C.L. exclusion sensitivity reach to the effective majorana neutrino mass $m_{\\beta\\beta}$ as a function of the lightest neutrino mass for normal (left) and inverted (right) neutrino mass hierarchy. The width of the horizontal bands derive from the uncertainty in nuclear matrix elements (see text) and it assumes that $g_A = 1.27$. The width of the inner dashed bands result from the unknown Majorana phases and is irreducible. The outer solid lines incorporate the 90\\% CL errors of the 3-flavor neutrino fit of reference \\cite{Forero-PhysRevD.90.093006,Tortola:comm}.","filename":"figures_sens_v_mass_nexo_10yrs_v9.png"}],"license":[{"url":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","imposing":"arXiv"}],"texkeys":["nEXO:2017nam","Albert:2017hjq"],"citeable":true,"imprints":[{"date":"2018-06-16"}],"keywords":[{"value":"double-beta decay: (0neutrino)","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"detector: design","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"proposed experiment","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"observatory","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"double-beta decay: (2neutrino)","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"performance","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"time projection chamber: xenon","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"xenon: liquid","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"background: low","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"xenon: nuclide","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"neutrino: Majorana","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"neutrino: Dirac","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"Skyrme model","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"neutrino: mass","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"numerical calculations","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"shell model","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"random phase approximation: quasiparticle","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"experimental results","schema":"INSPIRE"}],"refereed":true,"abstracts":[{"value":"The next-generation Enriched Xenon Observatory (nEXO) is a proposed experiment to search for neutrinoless double-β (0νββ) decay in Xe136 with a target half-life sensitivity of approximately 1028 yr using 5×103 kg of isotopically enriched liquid-xenon in a time projection chamber. This improvement of two orders of magnitude in sensitivity over current limits is obtained by a significant increase of the Xe136 mass, the monolithic and homogeneous configuration of the active medium, and the multiparameter measurements of the interactions enabled by the time projection chamber. The detector concept and anticipated performance are presented based upon demonstrated realizable background rates.","source":"APS"},{"value":"The next-generation Enriched Xenon Observatory (nEXO) is a proposed experiment to search for neutrinoless double beta ($0\\nu\\beta\\beta$) decay in $^{136}$Xe with a target half-life sensitivity of approximately $10^{28}$ years using $5\\times10^3$ kg of isotopically enriched liquid-xenon in a time projection chamber. This improvement of two orders of magnitude in sensitivity over current limits is obtained by a significant increase of the $^{136}$Xe mass, the monolithic and homogeneous configuration of the active medium, and the multi-parameter measurements of the interactions e